A||||9||Eric||Crazy Centerforce Clutch||||1||
Z||000000||Eric||12-10-1999||09:38 PM||||I just fired up my new engine today and drove the car around a little bit.  I bled the clutch system and found the pedal pressure to the brand new Centerforce Dual Friction clutch was very very light.  The strangest part was the clutch was functioning perfectly.  It engaged midway through the stroke, like it should, but the pedal remained very light and spongy feeling.  My old clutch required a strong left foot, in fact it was the hardest clutch pedal I have ever felt in a car.  Now the pedal feel is the lightest I've ever felt.  It's even lighter than my four banger Ford Ranger by about half.  People are telling me it's fine, it's a different clutch, it doesn't work the same as the old clutch.  I can't figure it out, so what's the deal?||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||12-11-1999||12:20 AM||||That's the beauty of a Centerforce clutch! It's a high performance clutch that require very little leg effort to push it in. Don't try to analyze it, it's magic. Just go with it. Be one with the clutch. May the Centerforce be with you!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||12-11-1999||12:58 PM||||Hahahahahaha!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shiner||12-11-1999||01:16 PM||||Are the centerforce clutches really that light?  I am going to be dropping the tranny on my '87 GT sometime to fix a bad 3rd gear synchro, and I might pop one of these babies in if it is REALLY that light! (the heavy clutch on my gt is bothersome in traffic)||||reg||10
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-11-1999||04:07 PM||||I have heard mixed reviews of the Centerforce.  Let me know what you think of it.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||12-11-1999||07:14 PM||||Well it still works.  There may be a little air in the system, but I really doubt that's what is making the pedal so light.  I could really get used to this.  Other people can now drive my car (I don't know if that's a good thing) and I won't have to beef up my "clutch muscles" like I previously thought.  I don't drag race, so the clutch won't get too much abuse.  I'll just give it a run every now and again for fun.||||reg||1
Z||000006||JIM GOOSE||12-14-1999||11:10 AM||||I've got the CF/DF in my car and the pedal pressure is light but clutch function is OK. Been in service about 5k miles with no problems.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Eric||12-14-1999||12:08 PM||||I don't know for sure, but it seems to me that the less pressure it takes to push in the clutch pedal, the less pressure you're putting on the slave cylinder, master cylinder and clutch line.  This thing could be the way around having to bleed your clutch every other day.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Richard Parnell||12-14-1999||01:49 PM||||Eric- Did you replace only the clutch or did you work on the flywheel also?  I replaced my clutch several years ago but it was only your run of the mill NAPA replacement.  Worked good for a couple of years but now as you may have noted in another tech question I've been going through hell with the hydraulics on the car.  I'm positive there is NO air in the system but it still won't shift properly.  I can pull the trans away from the engine in a couple of hours and switch that baby right out. I'd rather do that than fool around with that darned hydraulic anymore.  Has anyone ever thought of using a solenoid for the clutch throwout? ||||reg||5
Z||000009||BOSTITCH||12-14-1999||05:59 PM||||I don't really know maybe it is just light. but congratulations on the conversion completion I love mine hope you do to. I just thought I'd mention with my new clutch it was a bit spongy, bleeding the haynes way then my friend recommended v8 archie way which is basically add some leverage to assure air release then fully compress by hand the slave cylinder plunger tighten the cap then release. Seemed to work. Wish I had a CF though.||||reg||1
